				\begin{document}$$\% {\text{Hemolysis}} = \frac{{{\text{OD}}\left( {{\text{PEP}}} \right) - {\text{OD}}\left( {0.9\% {\text{PBS}}} \right)}}{{{\text{OD}}\left( {0.1\% {\text{Triton}} \times 100} \right) - {\text{OD}}\left( {0.9\% {\text{PBS}}} \right)}} \times 100\%$$\end{document}%Hemolysis=ODPEP-OD0.9%PBSOD0.1%Triton×100-OD0.9%PBS×100%where OD(x) is the optical density of each solution at 545 nm. The minimum hemolytic concentration (MHC) was determined as the lowest peptide concentration that induced 10% hemolysis of human red blood cells.
